  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1125 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Middle of Next Week: Dry and Warming\n\nAn Omega Block remains the dominant weather feature across the \nnorth central CONUS from today into the middle of next week. The\nridge temporarily weakens and rebuilds on Sunday, but the weak \nwave that ripples through the flow looks to stay well to the \nnorth and not impact the region. The 1028-mb surface high \npressure reflection that currently stretches from Hudson Bay to \nthe lower Missouri River Valley will likewise be very slow to \ndrift southeastward over the ensuing days, reaching the Lower \nGreat Lakes/upper Ohio River Valley by Monday. \n\nReturn SSW flow sets in across the region for Sunday and \nremains in place through at least the middle of next week. \nDespite the multiple days of southerly flow, we will be slow to \nwarm with Gulf air remaining relegated to the coast. Look for \nhighs to only rise 10-15 degrees between today and Wednesday \ngiven this setup. It is not until the Omega Block breaks down \nTuesday and Wednesday and lee cyclogenesis takes place along the\nFront Range that we see an appreciable degree of higher theta-e\nair stream northward. \n\n\nEnd of Next Week: Rain Returns, Cooling Off\n\nThe breakdown of the Omega Block for the middle to end of next \nweek ushers in substantial pattern change featuring a series of\nstronger shortwaves ejecting from a longwave trough that \nreplaces our current ridge over the Southwestern US. The first \nsuch wave is progged to arrive Wednesday night into Thursday \nfollowed by a second system for next weekend or possibly early \nthe week of Thanksgiving. \n\nAs one should expect at this range, there are major synoptic \ndifferences in the various traditional and AI ensembles that \nyield little for clues with the spatio-temporal details on the \nlocal impacts. The main message at this stage is that the \npattern will be turning more active towards the end of next \nweek. The main impacts look to come in the form of heavier \nrainfall with the mean LREF ensemble guidance already depicting \na 20-40% chance of over 1 inch of rainfall. In addition, colder \nair spills southward next weekend early into the week of \nThanksgiving with 20-30% of the longer range model guidance also\nhave measurable snow for this period.",
